---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import NoteIllustration from "../../../components/NoteIllustration.astro";
import NoteCover from "../../../components/NoteCover.astro";
import { SITE, notes } from "../../../data/site";

const note = notes.find((item) => item.slug === "production-got-cheap-judgment-didnt")!;

const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: note.title,
    description: note.summary,
    datePublished: note.date,
    author: {
      "@type": "Person",
      name: SITE.principal.name,
      url: SITE.principal.url
    },
    publisher: {
      "@type": "Organization",
      name: SITE.name,
      url: SITE.url
    }
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={note.title}
  description={note.summary}
  path={`/notes/${note.slug}/`}
  image={`/images/og/notes/${note.slug}.png`}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Notes · <time datetime={note.date}>{note.dateDisplay}</time></p>
    <h1>{note.title}</h1>
    <p>{note.summary}</p>
    <div class="note-hero-art"><NoteCover slug={note.slug} /></div>
  </section>

  <article class="article-body" data-analytics-component="article_body">
    <p>
      I built this site in about a week. It looks fine. Then I asked Google
      what it thought the site was, and Google told me I sell mayonnaise.
    </p>
    <p>
      Not a metaphor. Search the domain and one of the results is a product
      page for Hampton Creek Just Mayo, left over from whoever owned
      nicesalt.com before I did. Still sitting in the index, quietly
      competing with a consulting studio.
    </p>
    <p>
      Small problem, and a funny one. It points at a much less funny one.
    </p>

    <NoteIllustration kind="same-surface" />

    <p>
      Ten years ago a professional-looking site was proof of spend. Someone
      hired designers, wrote copy, paid a developer. Looking good was a
      signal because looking good was expensive. That signal is gone. A
      template or an afternoon with AI gets you something perfectly
      credible, and I'd know, because that's roughly what mine took.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the tools won't do is ask the three questions that decide whether
      the thing works.
    </p>

    <h2>Who is coming, and from where</h2>
    <p>
      Production answers what the site looks like. It never asks how anyone
      arrives. Most sites I look at have a confident answer to the first and
      no answer at all to the second, and the second is what determines
      whether the first matters.
    </p>

    <h2>What am I measuring, and is there anything to measure yet</h2>
    <p>
      Analytics is easy to install and easy to misread. I had GA4 and Search
      Console wired on day one, which felt responsible, and it told me
      nothing I couldn't have guessed until I went and read the index by
      hand. A dashboard reports what's happening on your site. It won't tell
      you what the internet already believes about your address.
    </p>

    <h2>What gets built next</h2>
    <p>
      DIY sites launch and freeze, because there's no evidence to build a
      roadmap from. A site that works is a sequence of small bets, each one
      shaped by what the last taught you. Over a year, the gap between that
      and a frozen site isn't cosmetic.
    </p>

    <NoteIllustration kind="next-bet" />

    <p>
      None of this is an argument against the tools. I use AI constantly:
      the build, illustration, motion, content production. It's why a small team
      can ship what used to take an agency, and I'm not giving that
      back.
    </p>
    <p>
      But the tools will happily help you build a beautiful answer to a
      question nobody asked, and they will never stop to ask who's coming.
      Mine let me publish a studio on top of a condiment.
    </p>
    <p class="article-cta" data-analytics-component="article_cta" data-analytics-event="cta">
      If your site looks fine and isn't doing much, the fix usually isn't
      another redesign. <a class="text-link" href="/free-teardown/" data-analytics-event="cta">Send
      me the link</a> and I'll tell you what I find.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
